<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Document xmlns:p="property" xmlns:r="reflection" Id="QFSq2LgyxQZKSOcKhJyYlS" LanguageVersion="2025.7.4" Version="0.128">
  <NugetDependency Id="DGYoYga2AUYCmaEZjUYGDx" Location="VL.CoreLib" Version="2025.7.4" />
  <Patch Id="Lv5zK0qheAfCHCDp2WfpIt">
    <Canvas Id="GwiEzbuSda9wDbrZmTJklM" DefaultCategory="Main" BordersChecked="false" CanvasType="FullCategory" />
    <!--

    ************************ Application ************************

-->
    <Node Name="Application" Bounds="100,100" Id="PYVnJC9NfB6F8wmxjikKxc">
      <p:NodeReference>
        <Choice Kind="ContainerDefinition" Name="Process" />
        <CategoryReference Kind="Category" Name="Primitive" />
      </p:NodeReference>
      <Patch Id="NA8y5F98CQ7Zr2WAYmsurR">
        <Canvas Id="Vr17y7UmX397fFM7K72qSd" CanvasType="Group">
          <Pad Id="RwwrdiiwLCZVtp5scIGJbQ" Bounds="50,30,900,200" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="VL.NetTopologySuite.GeoJSON&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;GeoJSON is the format most geographic data arrives in. This package is the GeoJSON half of VL.NetTopologySuite: three nodes - Read GeoJSON, GeoJSONReader (Reactive) and Write GeoJSON - in NetTopologySuite.IO beside Read WKT. No geometry maths here, no map, no reprojection.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;What comes out is NetTopologySuite's own Feature and Geometry, not a wrapper: VL.NetTopologySuite opens and computes with them, VL.Mapsui draws them.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation>
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">12</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="A46cdi80JTn4ewTRG5GpWp" Bounds="50,260,440,540" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The nodes - all three&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Read GeoJSON   text in, the features out, as a Spread. Also tells you whether it worked (Success), what went wrong if it did not (Status), and how many times it has actually parsed (Parses).&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;GeoJSONReader (Reactive)   a FILE in, read and parsed on another thread so vvvv keeps running. The features arrive on an observable; HoldLatest brings them in. Use it for anything bigger than a few hundred features.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Write GeoJSON   features back out as text. Exact Round Trip decides whether the coordinates come back byte for byte or get rewound to the RFC's winding rule.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Opening a feature is VL.NetTopologySuite's: Split gives its Geometry and its Attributes as a Dictionary, GetAttribute one by name with a Success pin. GeoJSON says properties, NetTopologySuite says attributes - the same table.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Everything takes an optional Factory pin where an SRID is involved. Leave it unconnected unless you know you want something else.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation>
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">11</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="Jt4rC5pSFnLEBZT9z3i7Ae" Bounds="520,260,440,680" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="What you will NOT find, and why&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;A FeatureCollection type. Read GeoJSON gives you the features directly, so there is no container to open first. That is how vvvv's own readers work: XMLReader and JSONReader take the document apart internally and hand a patch an XElement. A patch should never hold a &quot;document&quot;.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Because the result is a Spread, Count, GetSlice and a ForEach region all work on it - vvvv's own nodes, not ours. This package has no Count node for the same reason.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;A blocking file node. FileReader (String) [IO] into Read GeoJSON already is one. The one file node here exists because a big file must not freeze vvvv.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;A node you can build from nodes you already have is a help patch, not a node. A node browser you can read is worth more than one with everything in it.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;About SRID&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Geometry read here gets SRID 4326, because the GeoJSON standard says coordinates ARE longitude and latitude. VL.NetTopologySuite's Read WKT gives 0 instead, because plain WKT does not say what its numbers mean. Mixing the two never throws - it quietly gives a wrong answer - so if you combine them, use the Factory pin to make them agree. Setting an SRID is a label. It never moves a coordinate.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation>
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">11</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="KNumD3kR5ZiGV0C8zuqW6t" Bounds="50,830,900,320" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Where to go next&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;HowTo Read GeoJSON   start here. Text in one box, features out, and what happens when you break the text on purpose.&#xD;&#xA;HowTo Read a GeoJSON file   the same thing from disk, with vvvv's own FileReader.&#xD;&#xA;HowTo Access properties   the half of a feature that is not geometry. Two routes side by side - Split for all of them, GetAttribute for one by name, both from VL.NetTopologySuite - and what type each JSON value arrives as.&#xD;&#xA;HowTo Write GeoJSON   back out to text, and a round trip that proves nothing was lost on the way.&#xD;&#xA;HowTo Handle a large GeoJSON file   the one that matters if your data is real: GeoJSONReader (Reactive) beside a read that freezes vvvv, and the longest frame of each, measured in the patch.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Where geometry operations come from: VL.NetTopologySuite, which installs with this package. Area, Buffer, Intersection and the rest take exactly the Geometry Read GeoJSON produces.&#xD;&#xA;Where the map comes from: not here either. VL.Mapsui's FeatureLayer takes exactly the Features this package produces. Mapsui does not need to know this package exists, and that is the design working.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation>
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">11</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
        </Canvas>
        <Patch Id="Fozam4JWKB2tzOhCHONP7L" Name="Create" />
        <Patch Id="AL8eO2NjZzVmjtXFZNFzG4" Name="Update" />
        <ProcessDefinition Id="Pcus7s2AqyUfHmhUCrL4tc">
          <Fragment Id="HfT7p6EBoX486tEPPcthan" Patch="Fozam4JWKB2tzOhCHONP7L" Enabled="true" />
          <Fragment Id="I4S1L9X9G8mWFFmEv6VKmp" Patch="AL8eO2NjZzVmjtXFZNFzG4" Enabled="true" />
        </ProcessDefinition>
      </Patch>
    </Node>
  </Patch>
  <NugetDependency Id="JuwkkAztPrGPhJyRiVHgbg" Location="VL.NetTopologySuite.GeoJSON" Version="0.0.0" />
</Document>
